  "title": "Oxidation-Reduction Reactions Review",
  "content": {
    "bibrefs": [],
    "model": [
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Previously, you were introduced to oxidation-reduction reactions. Here we will delve more deeply into electrochemical processes, including many applications and connections to other areas of chemistry (thermodynamics and equilibrium). But first, let’s review some key concepts in electrochemistry that will be used throughout the unit."
              }
            ],
            "id": "ykru85tu6q4c17i",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "65nh1eyah3qf2s0",
                "text": "Oxidation-Reduction Reactions"
              }
            ],
            "id": "ih6vosj1k27uffa",
            "type": "h1"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Consider what happens when a clean piece of copper metal is placed in a solution of silver nitrate. The initially smooth copper wire becomes covered with a porous gray solid and the initially colorless solution becomes increasingly blue, as shown in the images below."
              }
            ],
            "id": "ln8f439cs0itrhv",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "alt": "This figure includes three photographs. In a, a test tube \n                    containing a clear, colorless liquid is shown with a loosely coiled copper wire outside the test tube to its right. \n                    In b, the wire has been submerged into the clear colorless liquid in the test tube and the surface of the wire is darkened. \n                    In c, the liquid in the test tube is a bright blue-green color, the wire in the solution appears dark near the top, and a \n                    grey “fuzzy” material is present at the bottom of the test tube on the lower portion of the copper coil, giving a murky \n                    appearance to the liquid near the bottom of the test tube.",
            "caption": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "(a) A clean piece of copper wire is placed into a clear, colorless solution of silver nitrate. (b) As the reaction proceeds, the solution turns blue and a silvery-grey solid metal is deposited on the surface of the wire (c). (credit: modification of work by Mark Ott)"
                  }
                ],
                "type": "p"
              }
            ],
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "0x9c5so5s8ofbcq",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "l64bd5iwczkeuu0",
            "src": "https://d2xvti2irp4c7t.cloudfront.net/media/fe/fe8d95e9895b18bd1b121ec6b0faf99f/cu_ag_redox_rxn.png",
            "type": "img",
            "width": "700"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "These observations are consistent with an oxidation-reduction reaction in which electrons are transferred from copper to silver. Elemental copper, Cu("
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "s"
              },
              {
                "text": "), \"loses\" electrons to form copper(II) ions, Cu"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "2+"
              },
              {
                "text": "("
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "aq"
              },
              {
                "text": "), which impart a blue color to the solution. Silver(I) ions, Ag"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "+"
              },
              {
                "text": "("
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "aq"
              },
              {
                "text": "), \"gain\" electrons to yield elemental silver, Ag("
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "s"
              },
              {
                "text": "), which deposits as a fluffy solid on the copper wire surface. A summary of what occurs is provided by these equations:"
              }
            ],
            "id": "x0utoux66ckc484",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "lcm2lr69anj4ejz",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "taagtjqgcqo4lyq",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "\\begin{array}{l}\\mathrm{oxidation}:\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\mathrm{Cu}(s) \\;\\;\\longrightarrow\\;\\;\\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}(aq)\\;+\\;2\\;\\mathrm e^-\\\\\\underline{\\mathrm{reduction}: \\;\\;\\;\\;\\;2\\;\\mathrm{Ag}^+(aq)\\;+\\;2\\;\\mathrm e^-\\;\\;\\longrightarrow\\;2\\;\\;\\mathrm{Ag}(s)\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\; \\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;}\\\\\\mathrm{overall}:\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;2\\;\\mathrm{Ag}^+(aq)\\;+\\;\\mathrm{Cu}(s)\\;\\; \\longrightarrow\\;\\;2\\;\\mathrm{Ag}(s)\\;+\\;\\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}(aq)\\end{array}",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "The term oxidation was originally used to describe an element reacting with oxygen to form an oxide, and reduction initially indicated oxygen being removed from an oxide to yield the element, but its meaning has evolved to refer to a broad and important reaction class known as oxidation-reduction (redox) reactions. Redox reaction are now defined in terms of transfer of electrons or change in oxidation state:"
              }
            ],
            "id": "5zioo0yymnl1g3n",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "ylh111ypezpupas",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "1ezs3a910n2bt9f",
            "src": "https://d2xvti2irp4c7t.cloudfront.net/media/bf/bff5de076a20e3effebd4adc25b1b667/green_line.png",
            "type": "img"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": " "
                  },
                  {
                    "strong": true,
                    "text": "oxidation = loss of electrons"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "5pthcdpod7q3ycf",
                "type": "p"
              }
            ],
            "id": "j43mb5b2r5b371s",
            "type": "callout"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": " "
                  },
                  {
                    "strong": true,
                    "text": "reduction = gain of electrons"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "ovgd07853s849pf",
                "type": "p"
              }
            ],
            "id": "ddxh9tweavnx4ep",
            "type": "callout"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": " "
                  },
                  {
                    "strong": true,
                    "text": "oxidation = increase in oxidation number"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "f6mgiq64ajslmpk",
                "type": "p"
              }
            ],
            "id": "95jrlhm28p4ds99",
            "type": "callout"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": " "
                  },
                  {
                    "strong": true,
                    "text": "reduction = decrease in oxidation number"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "615lqrqqb5y19xt",
                "type": "p"
              }
            ],
            "id": "op5cafvty9svzl0",
            "type": "callout"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "11m8nphnt4ux6q0",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "qkveh3o9760gzgc",
            "src": "https://d2xvti2irp4c7t.cloudfront.net/media/bf/bff5de076a20e3effebd4adc25b1b667/green_line.png",
            "type": "img"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "In the reaction above, copper loses electrons and its oxidation state increases (from 0 to +2), so copper is oxidized. Zinc gains electrons and its oxidation state decreases (from +2 to 0), so zinc is reduced. Viewed from a more active perspective, copper functions as a reducing agent (reductant), since it provides the electrons that silver gains (copper helps silver to undergo reduction). Likewise, silver functions as an oxidizing agent (oxidant), as it effectively removes electrons from copper (silver helps copper to undergo oxidation)."
              }
            ],
            "id": "xnj4v4rbn6krq8f",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "q8imgzpip0d5py0",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "8non4a8qw7t9ml9",
            "src": "https://d2xvti2irp4c7t.cloudfront.net/media/bf/bff5de076a20e3effebd4adc25b1b667/green_line.png",
            "type": "img"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": " "
                  },
                  {
                    "strong": true,
                    "text": "reducing agent = species that is oxidized"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "3h4renrv5shgxvp",
                "type": "p"
              }
            ],
            "id": "6qbvlm4sej75t3s",
            "type": "callout"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": " "
                  },
                  {
                    "strong": true,
                    "text": "oxidizing agent = species that is reduced"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "n4yxpe6ldk50s9m",
                "type": "p"
              }
            ],
            "id": "3m202r6ns85j80w",
            "type": "callout"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "zl25gf65k5cnslr",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "gtkc5wyamwnyfrv",
            "src": "https://d2xvti2irp4c7t.cloudfront.net/media/bf/bff5de076a20e3effebd4adc25b1b667/green_line.png",
            "type": "img"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Note that although oxidation and reduction occur simultaneously, we often find it useful to describe each process separately using half-reactions. For the copper-silver reaction, the equation for the reduction half-reaction had to be doubled so the number of electrons “gained” in the reduction half-reaction equaled the number of electrons “lost” in the oxidation half-reaction."
              }
            ],
            "id": "m6pupa81szl2ug2",
            "type": "p"
          }
        ],
        "id": "13857532",
        "selection": {
          "anchor": {
