        "type": "group"
      },
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "3qqvkyheli1p56l",
                "text": "Oxidation Numbers"
              }
            ],
            "id": "vjp034rum58rn1d",
            "type": "h1"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "When a substance (atom, molecule or ion) loses electrons, it becomes more positively charged and we say it is oxidized. When an atom, molecule or ion gains electrons, it becomes more negatively charged and we say it is reduced. In the reactions above, this gain and loss of electrons was relatively easy to identify from the change in charge when an ion was formed from an atom, or an atom from an ion. However, in many redox reactions the transfer of electrons is not complete or explicit, and it is less straightforward to determine what is oxidized and reduced. To deal with this, chemists have defined a bookkeeping system in which each atom in a substance is assigned an "
              },
              {
                "strong": true,
                "text": "oxidation number"
              },
              {
                "text": " (or "
              },
              {
                "strong": true,
                "text": "oxidation state"
              },
              {
                "text": ") indicating the \"charge\" the atom would have if all its bonds were completely ionic. For example, consider a molecule of hydrogen fluoride, HF. Although the H-F bond is (polar) covalent, we treat it as ionic and assign the shared bonding electrons to the more electronegative element, fluorine. In this assignment F has gained an electron and therefore has an oxidation number of -1, while H has lost an electron so its oxidation state is +1."
              }
            ],
            "id": "saudjqlujxt1p8m",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Although oxidation numbers are often hypothetical charges, by tracking how they change during a reaction we can determine the elements that are oxidized and reduced. The following guidelines are used to assign oxidation numbers to each element in a molecule or ion."
              }
            ],
            "id": "diw4meqm74qfqut",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "x2h0to0vk8eg2la",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "srwvylad9zm2c1i",
            "src": "https://d2xvti2irp4c7t.cloudfront.net/media/bf/bff5de076a20e3effebd4adc25b1b667/green_line.png",
            "type": "img"
          },
          {
            "children": [],
            "id": "a7u5fdq5zugyk05",
            "items": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "strong": true,
                    "text": "Determining Oxidation Numbers"
                  }
                ],
                "type": "dt"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "children": [
                          {
                            "text": "The oxidation number of an atom in an elemental substance is zero."
                          }
                        ],
                        "type": "li"
                      },
                      {
                        "children": [
                          {
                            "text": "The oxidation number of a monatomic ion is equal to the ion’s charge."
                          }
                        ],
                        "type": "li"
                      },
                      {
                        "children": [
                          {
                            "text": "Oxidation numbers for common nonmetals are usually assigned as follows: "
                          },
                          {
                            "children": [
                              {
                                "children": [
                                  {
                                    "text": "Hydrogen: +1 when combined with nonmetals; −1 when combined with metals"
                                  }
                                ],
                                "type": "li"
                              },
                              {
                                "children": [
                                  {
                                    "text": "Oxygen: −2 in most compounds; sometimes −1 (in peroxides, O"
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "sub": true,
                                    "text": "2"
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "sup": true,
                                    "text": "2−"
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "text": "); very rarely −1/2 (in superoxides, O"
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "sub": true,
                                    "text": "2"
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "sup": true,
                                    "text": "−"
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "text": "); positive values when combined with F (values vary)"
                                  }
                                ],
                                "type": "li"
                              },
                              {
                                "children": [
                                  {
                                    "text": "Halogens: −1 for F always; −1 for other halogens except when combined with oxygen or other halogens (positive oxidation numbers in these cases, varying values)"
                                  }
                                ],
                                "type": "li"
                              }
                            ],
                            "type": "ul"
                          },
                          {
                            "text": " "
                          }
                        ],
                        "type": "li"
                      },
                      {
                        "children": [
                          {
                            "text": "The sum of the oxidation numbers for all atoms in a molecule or polyatomic ion equals the charge on the molecule or ion."
                          }
                        ],
                        "type": "li"
                      }
                    ],
                    "type": "ul"
                  }
                ],
                "type": "dd"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "Note: The proper convention for reporting charge is to write the number first, followed by the sign (e.g., 2+), while oxidation number is written with the reversed sequence, sign followed by number (e.g., +2). This convention is intended to emphasize the distinction between these two related properties."
                      }
                    ],
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "type": "dd"
              }
            ],
            "type": "dl"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "6uuw5qdgj1oacxk",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "uzd4b8cpju1ulpv",
            "src": "https://d2xvti2irp4c7t.cloudfront.net/media/bf/bff5de076a20e3effebd4adc25b1b667/green_line.png",
            "type": "img"
          }
        ],
        "id": "664194427",
        "selection": {
          "anchor": {
            "offset": 0,
            "path": [
              0,
              0
            ]
          },
          "focus": {
            "offset": 0,
            "path": [
